hallenges they were facing  find out about what was going on in  their lives at the time that they  started their businesses and one of the  things that I realized pretty soon after  I got into the research for the book was  that women have gravitated toward the  net and I want to back up for a second  because at the time that I got the idea  for this book I was working at Adobe on  the e-business team there and one of the  things that I noticed was that a there  were a lot of women on the internet team  there and that be when women co-workers  left they are often weren't leaving to  go off to another job in another company  a lot of the time they were leaving to  go start a business of their own and in  a lot of instances it was an internet  businesses an internet business and  prior to working at Adobe ID worked it  into it on the quick and calm team and  there are actually a couple of folks  here tonight from the quick and calm  team and there I noticed something  similar a lot of women working on the  internet team and also the same trend  when women left in several instances  they would leave to go start an internet  business of their own and I think part  of why this stuck out in my mind is that  I'd also spend some time working in the  semiconductor industry and there there  were totally different gender  demographics not a lot of women working  in the semiconductor industry so at the  same time that I was thinking about this  I also started reading more books about  entrepreneurial activity and reading  books about things that investors are  doing in the technology arena and  started to notice that for the most part  these books were focusing on men I'm  thinking of books like e boys which  tells the story of the venture  capitalists at benchmark books like the  silicon boys in their Valley of dreams  looks like the new new thing which  profiles Jim Clark there's aol.com there  are a lot of these books out there and  given what I was experiencing in the  workplace I started to feel like there  was a piece of the story that hadn't  been written yet and decided that I  would take some time away from my job at  Adobe to try and write this book and  calm divas  the result of that when when I first dug  into some of the kind of numbers  research for the book I came across some  very interesting statistics that venture  one puts out and the statistics show  that that internet startups have a  higher percentage of women CEOs and a  higher percentage of women in top  management positions than other types of  startup companies and on each of your  chairs there is a handout if you look at  the second page there's a graph that  shows this trend that you know just  shows the number of women CEOs and women  in management positions that internet  startups versus non internet startups so  for me this kind of confirmed what I had  an intuitive sense about from what I'd  seen going on in the workplace so I  started to wonder about why this is why  was this happening and after giving it  some thought and talking to a lot of  people about it came up with four  reasons that I think helped to explain  this trend one certainly is the Internet  boom of the late 90s with the Internet  boom there was so much capital there was  a glut of capital available to  entrepreneurs and this opened up  opportunities for entrepreneurs that  didn't fit the typical mold it opened up  new opportunities for women  entrepreneurs it certainly opened up  opportunities for young entrepreneurs  and for first-time entrepreneurs so  that's I think one of the things that is  going on that explains this trend I  think another thing is that Internet  companies haven't been particularly tied  to traditional ways of working or  entrenched power structures for those of  you who raised your hand and said that  you do work at internet companies you've  probably seen that in some cases these  companies are not very bureaucratic or  hierarchical some of them are even  pretty 