 a fast writer and there  was a hard deadline or at least I  believed the deadline was hard I think  you know in retrospect I've learned that  I probably could have said oh I can't  make the deadline and the publisher  would have moved it for me but you know  that the pace that I had to keep up with  was was tight I wrote the book in a  little over five months and so you know  it meant every day I was sitting at the  computer pretty much all day long  researching and writing when I wasn't  out interviewing people so I think just  the pace that I had to maintain to  finish it by the due date that I had was  one of the most difficult parts  what did I tell Ashley well I told  Ashley that regardless of which business  idea she pursued that you know she was  she was gonna learn a lot and just also  to told her that if she's thinking this  way already at age 12 that she's going  to have so many great experiences in  life and that it's such a pleasure to  get an email like that and then I told  her some practical things like that she  probably should talk to her parents  about it and but that she might want to  check out her local chapter score and  things like that so  any more questions yeah the most  inspiring  a bit of information you back from these  something that really inspired you to  start your own company  huh let's see you know I don't know if  there was one you know kind of one  little nugget of information that i  found most inspirational I think what  for me was most inspirational was the  abundance of entrepreneurial activity  out there by women and having the  opportunity to write about the stories  of all different types of women starting  businesses and that for me was was most  inspiring and also seeing that these are  not women who are starting small  home-based businesses these are women  who are raising venture capital and  trying to build you know billion dollar  businesses so that that to me was also  very inspiring because a lot of people  when they think women run business they  think small business they think  home-based business and so I wanted to  tell a very different story from that  about women going out there with big  ambitions trying to start very big  companies  to begin with you know it was it was  really the combination of work having  worked at Adobe having worked it into it  having seen how many women were working  in the internet space and having a sense  that these women were going out some of  them were going out and starting  companies of their own and then the  disjunction between that and walking  into a bookstore and seeing a big  display with a boys all over it or a big  display with the silicon boys in their  Valley of dreams all over it I wanted a  book to be out there that would be a  book that I would want to read that  would be a book that would draw John me  in and give me role models and give me  examples that I could relate to in a way  that that would be easier for me than  say relating to to the e boys look oh I  didn't know what to do I didn't know  what to do i was completely clueless and  actually what i did was i did a search  on amazon and i found a book called how  to write a book proposal and i followed  the instructions i wrote the book  proposal and in retrospective realized  that it's writing a book proposals very  much like writing a business plan you  have a few pages about what the  company's about and then you have a lot  of pages about how it's going to be  marketable and you know how it's going  to make money and this kind of thing so  I wrote the book proposal and then got  got very lucky i sent it off to  mcgraw-hill and got a call back and they  said that they wanted to do the book and  offered me a contract and then I still  didn't know what to do so I went back to  amazon and i found a book called  negotiating a publishing contract which  i read and that book held my hand  through the contract negotiation and  then I just hunkered down and started  interviewing people and writing and yeah  so that's no that's how  happened so I didn't go about it the  normal way because the normal way is  that people and I've learned more about  what the normal way is now that I've  gone through with me the abnormal ways  that you know usually a writer has an  agent in the agent connects them with  the publisher and the agent takes care  of the contractual issues and that kind  of thing but I'm glad that I did it this  way because I feel like I learned a lot  more doing it this way so any more  questions  the factors that make women gravitate to  the net would also apply to people of  color the openness and other support  structures there are some support  structures I think there are more  support structures for women  entrepreneurs but there are also a lot  of support structures out there for  minority entrepreneurs in fact many of  the venture capital funds that are  targeting women led companies also tart  target minority led companies the fund  here in Palo Alto that i mentioned new  vista capital is a fun for women and  minority entrepreneurs so there are  definitely resources out there i would  love to see more resources out there i  might especially love to see more  african-american women I'm starting  Internet companies I you know kept an  eye open toward diversity in selecting  people 4.com divas and and so I think  that that's kind of the group where it  was pretty clear that there was much  less entrepreneurial activity happening  among african-american women compared to  other minority women so  any more questions thoughts i'm trying  to find role models would you ever  consider writing a children's book like  ashley becomes an entrepreneur ha that's  an interesting idea I'll think about  that one I think about that one there  are some good books out for girls on  starting businesses not a lot of these  books but there are a few that I've seen  and they're really fun books that's a  neat idea cuz I have a very good friend  Catherine d who writes books on self  esteem for girls and maybe I'll pitch  that idea to her and see if she want to  co-author it yeah yeah but i think you  know there's certainly room out there  for books for girls about business and  there's also room for books out there  right now to just on a mentorship I  think in friendship and how friends and  mentors can be instrumental to achieving  successes in life an area that sort of  black and you know um children as  they're growing up girls in particular  learning about business right business  and money right right yeah I mean I I  think that it's shocking that that even  now in many high schools and junior high  schools there's nothing taught about you  know kind of just life skills balancing  a checkbook making a salad you know  these kinds of things aren't taught  taught in schools normally and I know I  mean I I didn't learn how to balance a  checkbook and probably until I started  working it into it right so but there  are some organizations that are helping  to teach girls these skills there is one  that's in San Diego it's called  independent means and they have startup  camps for girls that are kind of like  getting Guy Kawasaki does these boot  camps it's kind of similar to that they  do start up camps for girls and each of  the girls in the camp writes a business  plan and then execute on that business  plan  and a really great program so there are  some of these out there and I think no  the more that can be done in that area  the better any more questions okay well  thanks everybody for coming it's great  to see this crowd here and I see a lot  of familiar faces and that's really nice  too
 

